findings.body:
The mark is there to catch a session that changed while the pre-lock plan ran. Decision 88 names the case: "a holder released during that plan's gh calls ... may have changed it without any wait". The comment in `runMergeGate` names it too. But `planMerge` takes the mark last, after it has read the session and after the git and gh calls:

```ts
const session = getSession(db, req.sessionId);
...
return { session, mark: sessionMark(db, session.id), sandbox, target, pullRequest, regate };
```

Suppose another run rejects or merges the session during `findOpenPullRequest` / `planRegate`, then releases the lock. This run takes the lock without waiting. The mark it stored already includes the change, so `sessionMark(db, req.sessionId) !== planned.mark` is false, and the stale `planned.session` (still `awaiting-review`) goes into `gateAndMerge`. `gateAndMerge` never re-reads the state before `fastForward` (or `pushBranch` with `--pr`). The first check is `transitionSession(..., 'merged')`, which throws for `rejected→merged` only after main has moved. That is the outcome decision 88 says it prevents. When this run fails instead, `rejectOrBlock` counts a second rejection.

Take the mark before anything else is read, so any later change differs under the lock:

```ts
function planMerge(db: Database, req: MergeRequest): MergePlan {
  const mark = sessionMark(db, req.sessionId);
  const session = getSession(db, req.sessionId);
  ...
  return { session, mark, sandbox, target, pullRequest, regate };
```

The test at `merge-gate.test.ts:2509` changes the session at the take's pid write, which comes after the mark, so it does not cover this window. A variant that changes the session inside the `findOpenPullRequest` mock would.

<https://github.com/AlexisBalayre/pupitre/blob/899e73785a900275bcb3ed398743e51bddf483a5/src/core/merge-gate.service.ts#L637-L709>

